<h3>Who This Framework Is For</h3>

<p>The Local Authority Hub Framework is built for home service contractors: HVAC technicians, plumbers, roofers, electricians, landscapers, remodelers, painters, and general contractors. It works for single-location businesses and multi-location operations. It works in competitive metro markets and smaller service areas. The principles are universal because they are rooted in how Google's local algorithm actually works, not in tricks or loopholes that disappear with the next update.</p>

<p>If you are currently spending money on <a href="/services/lead-generation/">lead generation</a> services, home advisor platforms, or pay-per-click ads and you want to own your own lead flow, this framework is for you. If you are already doing some SEO but your results have plateaued, this framework will show you exactly what to fix. If you are starting from zero, this framework gives you a complete roadmap from day one.</p>

<h2>Phase 1: Foundation (Days 1-30)</h2>

<p>Phase 1 is about fixing what is broken and building what is missing. Most contractors we work with have some local SEO assets in place, but those assets are incomplete, inconsistent, or outdated. Phase 1 creates a solid base that every subsequent phase depends on. Skip this phase and everything else becomes harder, slower, and less effective.</p>

<p>The foundation phase has four workstreams: technical setup, Google Business Profile optimization, NAP consistency, and tracking infrastructure. Each workstream has specific tasks, tools, and completion criteria.</p>

<h3>Week 1: Technical Audit and Cleanup</h3>

<p>Your website is the spoke that carries the most weight after your GBP. If your site is slow, broken, or poorly structured, Google will not trust it enough to send local searchers your way. Week 1 is a full technical audit followed by immediate fixes.</p>

<p>Start with a crawl using Screaming Frog or Sitebulb. Identify and fix broken links, missing title tags, duplicate meta descriptions, and pages with no H1 headings. Check your site speed using Google PageSpeed Insights. If your mobile score is below 70, that is your first priority. According to Google, 78% of local mobile searches lead to an offline purchase. A slow mobile site kills conversions before they start.</p>

<p>Next, verify your site is mobile-responsive. Use Google's Mobile-Friendly Test. Then check your Core Web Vitals in Google Search Console. If your Largest Contentful Paint is above 2.5 seconds, your Time to First Byte is above 800 milliseconds, or your Cumulative Layout Shift is above 0.1, those are fixable issues that directly impact local rankings.</p>

<p>Finally, implement HTTPS if you have not already. Install an SSL certificate and redirect all HTTP URLs to HTTPS. This is basic hygiene in 2026, but we still see contractor sites without it.</p>

<h3>Week 2: Google Business Profile Overhaul</h3>

<p>Your Google Business Profile is the single most important asset in local search. GBP signals account for 32% of Map Pack ranking weight, more than any other factor. Yet 41% of claimed profiles remain incomplete. Week 2 is about making your profile the most complete and active one in your market.</p>

<p>Begin by verifying every field is filled. Primary category, secondary categories, business description, services, attributes, hours, phone number, website URL, appointment link, and service area. The primary category is the most important selection you will make. Choose the most specific category that describes your core service. If you are an HVAC contractor, "HVAC Contractor" is better than "Contractor." If you are a plumber, "Plumber" is better than "Plumbing Supply Store."</p>

<p>Write your business description using natural language that includes your primary service and primary city, but do not keyword stuff. Google penalizes profiles with spammy descriptions. Aim for 750 characters that tell a prospective customer who you are, what you do, and why they should call you.</p>

<p>Add at least 20 high-quality photos. Include images of your team, your trucks, your completed work, and your office or shop. Geotag your photos with your service area coordinates before uploading. Photos with geotags send stronger local relevance signals than unlabeled images. According to Google, businesses with photos receive 42% more requests for directions and 35% more click-throughs to their websites.</p>

<p>Set up Google Posts and commit to posting at least once per week. Posts expire after seven days, so consistency matters more than perfection. Share promotions, seasonal tips, project highlights, and company news. Posts keep your profile active and give Google fresh content to index.</p>

<h3>Week 3: NAP Consistency and Citation Cleanup</h3>

<p>NAP stands for Name, Address, Phone. Consistent NAP data across the web is a foundational trust signal. When Google sees the same business name, address, and phone number on your website, your GBP, Yelp, Angi, the Better Business Bureau, and dozens of other platforms, it gains confidence that you are a real, established business. Inconsistent NAP data creates confusion and dilutes your authority.</p>

<p>Start by documenting your exact NAP format. Decide whether you use "Street" or "St," whether you include suite numbers, and how you format your phone number. Write it down and never deviate.</p>

<p>Then audit your existing citations. Use BrightLocal, Moz Local, or Whitespark to scan the major directories and data aggregators. Identify every instance where your NAP does not match your documented format. Prioritize the top 30 platforms: Google, Yelp, Angi, BBB, HomeAdvisor, Facebook, Apple Maps, Bing Places, Nextdoor, Thumbtack, Houzz, Porch, and the major data aggregators (Data Axle, Neustar Localeze, Foursquare).</p>

<p>Fix the incorrect listings one by one. This is tedious work, but it is non-negotiable. A single wrong phone number on a high-authority directory can split your ranking signals and confuse potential customers. According to BrightLocal, 62% of consumers would avoid a business if they found incorrect information online.</p>

<h3>Week 4: Tracking and Analytics Setup</h3>

<p>You cannot improve what you do not measure. Week 4 is about installing the tracking infrastructure that tells you whether your 90-day investment is paying off.</p>

<p>Set up Google Analytics 4 with conversion events for phone clicks, form submissions, and appointment bookings. Install Google Search Console and verify ownership of your domain. Connect your GBP to Search Console so you can see how your profile performs in local search.</p>

<p>Implement call tracking using a service like CallRail or CallTrackingMetrics. Use dynamic number insertion so you can attribute calls to specific traffic sources. Without call tracking, you are guessing which leads came from organic search, paid ads, or referrals.</p>

<p>Set up a rank tracking tool. Local Falcon, BrightLocal, or Whitespark's rank tracker will show you where you appear in the Map Pack for specific keywords at specific locations. Rank tracking by zip code or neighborhood matters more than city-level tracking because the Map Pack results change based on the searcher's exact location.</p>

<p>Finally, create a simple dashboard that tracks your four key metrics: Map Pack position for your top 10 keywords, total GBP actions (calls, directions, website clicks), total reviews and average star rating, and total qualified leads attributed to organic search. Update this dashboard weekly. It is your scoreboard for the next 60 days.</p>

