<h2>FAQ: The Local Authority Hub Framework</h2>

<h3>What is a local SEO framework?</h3>

<p>A <strong>local SEO framework</strong> is a structured, repeatable system for improving a business's visibility in local search results. Unlike a random checklist of tactics, a framework defines the sequence, timing, and dependencies of each action so that every step builds on the one before it. The Local Authority Hub Framework uses a three-phase, 90-day structure: Foundation, Authority, and Domination.</p>

<h3>How long does it take to see results from local SEO?</h3>

<p>Most contractors see measurable improvements within 30 to 45 days if they execute the Foundation phase correctly. Map Pack rankings typically improve between days 45 and 75, depending on market competition. Lead volume increases usually follow ranking improvements by 2 to 4 weeks. The full 90-day timeline is realistic for most home service markets.</p>

<h3>Can I do local SEO myself, or do I need an agency?</h3>

<p>You can execute the Local Authority Hub Framework yourself if you have 8 to 12 hours per week and basic technical comfort. The Foundation phase requires the most technical skill. The Authority and Domination phases are more about consistency and relationship building. If you prefer to focus on running your business, an agency can execute the framework while you review weekly dashboards and approve content.</p>

<h3>How much does local SEO cost for a contractor?</h3>

<p>DIY local SEO costs $500 to $1,500 per month in tools and software. Agency-managed local SEO for contractors typically ranges from $1,500 to $3,500 per month depending on market size, competition, and service area coverage. The cost-per-lead from mature local SEO averages $15 to $60, compared to $104 to $228 for Google Search Ads.</p>

<h3>What is the most important ranking factor for local SEO?</h3>

<p>According to Whitespark's 2026 Local Ranking Factors Survey, Google Business Profile signals account for 32% of Map Pack ranking weight, the single largest factor. Within GBP signals, the primary category, proximity to the searcher, and keywords in the business title carry the most weight.</p>

<h3>How many reviews do I need to rank in the Map Pack?</h3>

<p>The average top-three Map Pack result has 561 reviews with a 4.8-star rating. However, review velocity matters more than total count. A business with 50 reviews that adds 10 per month will outrank a business with 200 reviews that has not added a new one in six months. Aim for 15 to 25 new reviews per month during your authority-building phase.</p>

<h3>What is NAP consistency, and why does it matter?</h3>

<p>NAP stands for Name, Address, Phone. NAP consistency means your business name, address, and phone number are identical across every platform where your business appears: your website, Google Business Profile, Yelp, Angi, BBB, and every other directory. Inconsistent NAP data confuses Google, splits your ranking signals, and causes 62% of consumers to avoid your business.</p>

<h3>Do I need a website if I have a Google Business Profile?</h3>

<p>Yes. Your GBP can generate calls and directions on its own, but your website is where conversions happen. Google also uses your website content to validate your GBP information. Businesses with websites linked to their GBP receive 35% more website clicks than those without. Your website is also essential for ranking in organic search results below the Map Pack.</p>

<h3>How do I optimize for AI search engines like ChatGPT?</h3>

<p>AI search engines pull from multiple data sources, not just Google. To optimize for AI visibility, ensure your NAP is consistent across every platform, add FAQ schema to your website, publish clear factual content that answers specific questions, and maintain active profiles on Yelp, Apple Maps, Bing Places, and other major platforms. AI visibility is 30 times harder to achieve than Google local rankings, so consistency across the web is critical.</p>

<h3>What is geo-grid expansion?</h3>

<p>Geo-grid expansion is the process of improving your Map Pack visibility across your entire service area, not just the neighborhoods closest to your office address. It involves creating location-specific content for outlying neighborhoods, earning backlinks from local organizations in those areas, and adding geo-tagged photos from jobs in those locations to your GBP.</p>

<h3>How do I track whether my local SEO is working?</h3>

<p>Track four metrics weekly: Map Pack position for your top 10 keywords, total GBP actions (calls, directions, website clicks), total reviews and average star rating, and total qualified leads attributed to organic search. Use rank tracking tools like Local Falcon, call tracking like CallRail, and dashboards in Google Looker Studio to monitor these metrics.</p>

<h3>What are the biggest mistakes contractors make with local SEO?</h3>

<p>The three biggest mistakes are: treating local SEO as a one-time project instead of an ongoing system, creating thin or duplicate location pages that add no unique value, and measuring traffic instead of leads and revenue. Other common errors include inconsistent NAP data, ignoring negative reviews, and failing to post regularly on their GBP.</p>

<h3>Should I target every city in my service area at once?</h3>

<p>No. Start with your highest-revenue market, the city where you already have the most customers and the strongest reputation. Dominate that market first, then expand to adjacent cities one at a time. Spreading your efforts too thin across ten cities at once dilutes your signals and delays results in every market.</p>

<h3>How often should I post on my Google Business Profile?</h3>

<p>Post at least once per week. Google Posts expire after seven days, so weekly posting ensures your profile always has fresh content. Post about promotions, seasonal tips, project highlights, and company news. Businesses that post weekly see higher engagement rates than those that post sporadically.</p>

<h3>What is the difference between local SEO and traditional SEO?</h3>

<p>Traditional SEO focuses on ranking in organic search results for broad keywords. Local SEO focuses on ranking in the Map Pack and local organic results for location-specific queries. Local SEO depends heavily on Google Business Profile signals, review signals, NAP consistency, and local backlinks. Traditional SEO depends more on domain authority, content depth, and national backlink profiles.</p>

<h2>Your 30-Day Action Plan</h2>

<p>You now have the complete Local Authority Hub Framework. Here is what to do in the next 30 days to start building momentum.</p>

<p><strong>Week 1:</strong> Run a technical audit of your website. Fix broken links, slow pages, and mobile issues. Verify HTTPS is active.</p>

<p><strong>Week 2:</strong> Overhaul your Google Business Profile. Fill every field, choose the right categories, add 20+ geotagged photos, and write a complete business description.</p>

<p><strong>Week 3:</strong> Audit your NAP consistency across the top 30 directories. Fix every inconsistency. Document your exact NAP format and never deviate.</p>

<p><strong>Week 4:</strong> Install call tracking, set up Google Analytics 4 conversion events, and create your weekly metrics dashboard.</p>

<p>If you complete these four weeks, you will have a stronger local search foundation than 80% of your competitors. That is not an exaggeration. BrightLocal's data shows that 65% of small businesses have never claimed their GBP, and 41% of claimed profiles remain incomplete. Simply showing up completely and consistently puts you in the top tier.</p>

<p>From there, Phase 2 builds the authority signals that separate the top three from everyone else. Phase 3 turns those signals into defensible market dominance. The entire framework is designed to be executed in 90 days, measured in real business outcomes, and maintained with a few hours per week.</p>

<p>Local search is not a mystery. It is a system. And the contractors who treat it as a system are the ones who win.</p>

